# The fixed terms of the translations: one concept, one word, in every language, so a later pass says the same thing the same way.
# A line is the English concept, then the word in es, fr, de, pt, it, nl, pl, tr, sv, ru, uk, el, ar, he, fa, ur, separated by " | ".
# Add a line before using a new term twice; change a line only with every text that uses it.
#
# Rules that hold for every text:
#   - Never translated: Moonwater, Canvas, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, USB, NTS, TLS, SMT, MiB, ms, Ctrl, Alt, Shift, Enter and Escape
#     where they name a key, rrggbb, and every {name} of a format. A word a person types (default, auto, off, reset, on)
#     stays English inside a label that tells them to type it, and is translated where the window only shows it.
#   - Placeholders (%s, %d, %1$s) and {names} are kept whole; a translation may put them in another order, naming them (%2$s).
#   - A mnemonic is & before one ASCII letter; Cyrillic, Greek, Arabic, Hebrew, Persian and Urdu texts have none, and no menu has two the same.
#   - One line of UTF-8, short: the windows cut with "..." where it does not fit. German and Russian run 30 to 40 percent over English.
#   - Brazilian Portuguese and Latin American neutral Spanish where the two regions differ. Formal "you" where a text addresses one.
#   - Machine translations: marked @translated machine in the file, until a person who speaks the language has reviewed it.
#   - ar, he, fa and ur read from the right: their files say @direction rtl, and no other file does. They keep ASCII digits 0-9 and the
#     Latin of what the person types (moonwater, Ctrl+R, default); they write the language's own punctuation (ar, fa, ur: a comma, a semicolon
#     and a question mark of their own, and ur ends a sentence with the Arabic full stop); no text ends in a Latin placeholder followed by punctuation,
#     which the line would set on the wrong side. Persian puts a ZWNJ (U+200C) where its spelling has one (می‌خواند, ها). ar: modern standard
#     Arabic; he: standard Modern Hebrew; fa: standard Iranian Persian; ur: standard Urdu of Pakistan.
#
